DUT SUMMARY

Private Technical Summary

Project: AXI4-Lite to APB Bridge (SystemVerilog)

1. Project Overview

This project implements an AXI4-Lite to APB bridge in SystemVerilog.
The goal was to enable an AXI master to access APB peripherals while strictly respecting both protocol specifications.

AXI and APB differ fundamentally:

AXI is decoupled and channel-based

APB is transaction-based and sequential

The core challenge was to bridge these timing models safely, without protocol violations, deadlocks, or data loss.

2. Architecture Decisions
2.1 Separation of AXI and APB Domains

The architecture intentionally decouples AXI-side logic from APB-side logic using:

FSMs

A command packer

A FIFO

This prevents APB timing from directly affecting AXI handshakes and vice versa.

Key principle:

Never let downstream timing control upstream protocol handshakes.

2.2 AXI Read and Write FSMs

Separate FSMs were created for:

AXI write channel

AXI read channel

Why FSMs were required:

AXI address, data, and response channels are independent

Handshakes may occur in different cycles

Pulse-based logic is insufficient

FSMs track transaction progress, not just signal pulses.

2.3 Command Packer

The command packer collects AXI handshakes and emits a single internal command.

Why this block exists:

AXI is channel-oriented

APB is transaction-oriented

A translation layer is mandatory

The packer converts:

AW + W → WRITE command

AR → READ command

Design rule followed:

Handshakes are events, commands are state.

2.4 FIFO (Depth = 1)

A single-entry FIFO sits between AXI and APB.

Why FIFO depth = 1:

APB supports only one active transaction

The design enforces single outstanding transaction

Simplifies verification and control logic

Even at depth = 1, the FIFO provides:

Ordering

Backpressure

Clean handshaking

2.5 Arbitration Strategy

Arbitration is handled structurally:

Write has priority over read when both are pending

This avoids:

Write data buffering issues

Complex reordering logic

The priority decision is intentional and documented, not accidental.

3. Major Bugs Fixed (Critical Learnings)
3.1 Pulse vs State Bug (Primary Issue)

Problem:

cmd_valid was initially generated as a combinational pulse

FIFO missed the pulse if not ready

Entire design stalled silently

Root cause:

Treating protocol events (pulses) as state

Fix:

Latch command validity using registers

Hold cmd_valid until cmd_ready is observed

Key learning:

In hardware, pulses are not memory. State must be explicit.

3.2 Gating AXI Capture with Internal Control

Problem:

AXI handshakes were initially captured only when internal enable signals were high

This violated AXI protocol assumptions

Fix:

Capture AW/W/AR strictly on VALID && READY

Internal logic must never gate protocol capture

Learning:

Protocols define ownership of signals — internal logic must adapt, not override.

3.3 Read/Write Response Ambiguity

Problem:

A single response signal was triggering both read and write FSMs

Fix:

Explicitly tag responses with command type

Route responses correctly

3.4 Verification Monitor Bug (AXI Semantics)

Problem:

Testbench assumed AW and W handshake in the same cycle

Missed valid writes

Fix:

Track AW and W independently in monitors

Declare write handshake complete only after both are seen

Learning:

Verification logic must follow protocol semantics, not assumptions.

4. Verification Strategy

Verification was done using:

Directed SystemVerilog testbench

Self-checking monitors

Counters and assertions

Waveform inspection

Key cases verified:

Back-to-back writes

Back-to-back reads

Read-after-write

AW/W separated in time

Simultaneous read and write arrival

Backpressure behavior

The design was also synthesized for a supported FPGA device to confirm synthesizability.

5. Why Certain Choices Were Made
Single Outstanding Transaction

Chosen to:

Reduce complexity

Avoid ID tracking

Match AXI-Lite behavior

Improve reliability

No Burst Support

Out of scope by design.
Focus was correctness and protocol understanding, not throughput.

Simple APB Slave Model

Used only for verification clarity, not functional completeness.

